          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In pemphigus vulgaris, which cellular structure is targeted by autoantibodies, l</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In pemphigus vulgaris, which cellular structure is targeted by autoantibodies, leading to intra-epidermal blistering?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hemidesmosomes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hemidesmosomes anchor basal keratinocytes to the basement membrane. Their destruction causes sub-epidermal blistering, as seen in bullous pemphigoid — not pemphigus vulgaris.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Desmosomes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. IgG autoantibodies attack desmosomal cadherins, breaking the intercellular bridges between keratinocytes in the stratum spinosum and causing acantholysis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gap junctions</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gap junctions allow intercellular communication but do not provide structural adhesion between keratinocytes. Their loss does not produce blistering.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tight junctions</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Tight junctions form paracellular seals primarily in simple epithelia. They are not the target in pemphigus vulgaris.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The target is desmosomes. In pemphigus vulgaris, IgG autoantibodies destroy desmosomal proteins, causing the keratinocytes of the stratum spinosum to lose adhesion — a process called acantholysis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! Desmosomes are the key cell–cell adhesion structures in the epidermis, and their autoimmune destruction is the hallmark of pemphigus vulgaris."
                }
              ],
