          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During karyotyping, colchicine is added to the cell culture. What is its specifi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During karyotyping, colchicine is added to the cell culture. What is its specific role in the procedure?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It stimulates lymphocyte division</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lymphocyte division is stimulated by phytohaemagglutinin (PHA), a plant-derived mitogen added at the start of the culture — not by colchicine.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It causes cells to swell so chromosomes spread apart</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cell swelling is caused by the hypotonic potassium chloride solution added after colchicine treatment, not by colchicine itself.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It arrests cell division at metaphase by blocking spindle formation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Colchicine inhibits tubulin polymerisation, preventing spindle fibre assembly and trapping cells in metaphase when chromosomes are maximally condensed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It produces the banding pattern used for chromosome identification</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Banding patterns are produced by staining techniques — most commonly G-banding with Giemsa stain — applied after the chromosome spreads are prepared.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Colchicine blocks spindle fibre formation, arresting cells at metaphase — the stage when chromosomes are maximally condensed and suitable for analysis. Do not confuse its role with that of PHA (stimulates division), hypotonic KCl (swells cells), or Giemsa stain (banding)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! Colchicine arrests mitosis at metaphase, the stage when chromosomes are most condensed and can be individually identified in the karyogram."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
